<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#roman-citizenship">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman citizenship</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as civitas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman citizenship, civitas, was the privileged political and legal status of free individuals in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, governing their rights in law, property and government. There were several kinds, shaped by gender, class and political ties, and citizens owed duties to the state, such as military service, and were recorded in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> every five years under the Republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-tables">Twelve Tables</a> of about 449 BCE are the oldest surviving document of its rights. Citizenship was extended to the Italian allies around the Social War, and in 212 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutio-antoniniana">Edict of Caracalla</a> gave it to all free men of the empire, with some exceptions.</p></div>
